+### Durations
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string such as `90s`, `1h30m` or `1m500ms` into a `datetime.timedelta`. A value
+is one or more integer parts, each followed by a unit: `d`, `h`, `m`, `s` or `ms`. The units must go from largest to
+smallest, and each may appear at most once. A bare integer is a number of seconds. Negative values and values with
+spaces are rejected.
+
+`min` and `max` are optional inclusive bounds, given as `timedelta` objects, and work like the bounds of
+{class}`IntRange`.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    from datetime import timedelta
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--wait", type=click.Duration(timedelta(seconds=5), timedelta(minutes=30)), default="30s")
+    def sleep(wait):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {wait}")
+```
+
